var e=[{kind:`lesson`,id:`B1`,track:`ble`,title:`Listen`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Hear every Bluetooth device around you`,goal:`Phones, watches, earbuds, speakers, keyboards and beacons announce themselves all day: short <b>advertising</b> packets on three radio channels, many times a second, whether anyone listens or not. B1 turns the board into a listener. It draws them on a radar, guesses what each one is, and takes a packet apart byte by byte. The radio uses NimBLE, a lightweight Bluetooth stack; the firmware is its own (<code>ble-lab-b1</code>), with shared code in <code>kit/lib/ble-kit</code>.`,goalLine:`Find your own phone among the devices around you, read its packets byte by byte, and measure how often it advertises.`,goalCheck:[`The <b>radar</b> view shows blips and the monitor's <code>list</code> prints a table of devices with a kind (phone, audio, keyboard…).`,`You picked your own phone (bring it close: it becomes the strongest) and <code>bytes</code> shows its AD structures: Flags, then manufacturer data (Apple, Samsung, Google…).`,`<code>watch</code> prints one line per packet of your phone, and you can read its advertising interval from the <code>+… ms</code> gaps.`,`Switching <code>mode passive</code> makes the scan responses (and some names) disappear; <code>mode active</code> brings them back.`],learn:[`advertising`,`channels 37 · 38 · 39`,`AD structures`,`active vs passive scan`,`RSSI → distance`,`private addresses`,`scan duty cycle`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b1 -e ble-lab-b1 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b1 -e ble-lab-b1 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Builds lab B1 and writes it to the board. The first build downloads NimBLE-Arduino 2.5.1 (the Bluetooth stack).`,see:`<code>Hard resetting via RTS pin…</code>. The screen shows the radar with a sweeping line.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`uv run pio device monitor`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Opens the serial monitor (the board restarts).`,see:`<code>ble-lab-b1: NimBLE 2.5.1 up, address …</code>, <code>+ active scan, 100/100 ms (100% listening)</code>, then the command list.`,fix:`No <code>NimBLE … up</code> line: the stack didn't start; unplug and replug the board.`},{command:`list`,card:`bscan`,does:`Prints every device heard in the last 30 s, strongest first: RSSI, a distance guess, kind, address and its type, packets, name or maker.`,see:`A table such as <code>1  -46  0.6  phone  5A:3C:…  private (RPA)  412  Apple</code>. Ten to fifty rows in a flat, fewer in a house.`,fix:`Empty: is Bluetooth on around you? Phones often advertise only when their screen is on or an app wants them to.`},{command:`(hold your phone next to the board)`,where:`hands`,card:`bscan`,does:`Your phone becomes the strongest device. Unlock it: iPhones advertise more often with the screen on.`,see:`On the <b>radar</b>, one blip moves to the centre; on <b>list</b> it jumps to row 1 with about −40 dBm.`,fix:`Android phones may stay silent: open nRF Connect and use its Advertiser tab, or switch on Nearby Share / Quick Share for a moment.`},{command:`pick 1, then bytes`,card:`bscan`,does:`Picks row 1 for the <b>inspect</b> view and prints its advertisement and scan response, structure by structure.`,see:`<code>[02 01 | 1A] Flags</code>, <code>[0A FF | 4C 00 10 …] Manufacturer data</code> (Apple: Nearby info), maybe <code>[02 0A | 0C] TX power</code>.`,fix:`<code>no scan response</code>: some devices don't send one; in passive mode nobody asks for it.`},{command:`watch`,card:`bscan`,does:`Prints one line per packet of the picked device, with the time since the previous one.`,see:`<code>&lt; +  182 ms  -47 dBm  31 B</code> lines. Some gaps are missed packets (your phone sent it on a channel the board wasn't listening on); the shortest repeating gap is its interval.`,fix:`<code>watch off</code> stops it. Too many lines: <code>near -60</code> first.`},{command:`mode passive, then mode active`,card:`bscan`,does:`Passive: the board only listens. Active: it also sends a scan request to every scannable device and gets a second packet back, the scan response.`,see:`In passive mode the <b>inspect</b> view loses its <i>scan rsp</i> row and some names turn into makers. The air view shows the mode.`,fix:``},{command:`timing 100 30`,card:`bscan`,does:`Listen 30 ms out of every 100 (30 % duty cycle) instead of all the time.`,see:`Packets per second on the <b>air</b> view drop to about a third. Phones scan like this to save battery. <code>timing 100 100</code> goes back.`,fix:`Window must be ≤ interval.`},{command:`calib (phone 1 m away)`,card:`bscan`,does:`Takes the picked device's average RSSI as the 1 m reference for the distance guesses.`,see:`<code>+ 1 m = -58 dBm now: distances use it</code>. Walk away: the blip drifts outwards.`,fix:`Distances jump by ±1 m: that's RSSI. Bodies, walls and the phone's orientation move it by 5–10 dB.`}],panels:[{title:`The Bluetooth track, from listening to hosting`,widget:`bleJourney`,intro:`Eight labs, each its own firmware. The board starts as a pure listener and ends up hosting your keyboard. Pick a lab to see the board's role and which way the data flows; the lock marks the labs where pairing and encryption matter.`},{title:`Three channels, many shouts`,widget:`bleRadio`,intro:`Bluetooth LE splits the 2.4 GHz band into 40 channels of 2 MHz. Advertising uses only three of them, 37, 38 and 39, placed in the gaps between the usual Wi-Fi channels 1, 6 and 11. Every advertising event sends the same packet on all three, one after the other. The scanner listens on one channel at a time: a packet counts only if it lands inside a listening window on its channel. Move the window slider and watch how many packets the board catches; switch to active to see the scan request and response.`},{title:`One advertising packet, byte by byte`,widget:`bleAnatomy`,intro:`What a radio actually sends, from the first bit to the checksum. Pick a device, then step through the fields with ◀ ▶. The AD structures in the middle are exactly what the <b>inspect</b> view and the <code>bytes</code> command show.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`bleScreens`,intro:`Four views. A click on the push button (or BOOT) goes to the next one, a double-click runs the action written at the bottom left, a long press pauses or resumes the scan. The icon at the top right is the radio: a sweeping radar while it scans.`}],steps:[`Keep the screen wired as in lesson 03 (push button optional). Flash <code>ble-lab-b1</code> and open the monitor. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> <code>NimBLE 2.5.1 up</code> and the radar sweeping.`,`Type <code>list</code>. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> a table of devices with kinds; look for <i>audio</i> (your JBL advertises for its app), <i>keyboard/mouse</i> and Apple or Microsoft makers.`,`Hold your unlocked phone next to the board, <code>pick 1</code>, click to <b>inspect</b>. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> its bytes in coloured AD structures, the address type is <i>private (RPA)</i>.`,`Type <code>watch</code> for ten seconds, then <code>watch off</code>. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> you can name your phone's advertising interval.`,`Try <code>mode passive</code> / <code>mode active</code> and <code>timing 100 30</code> / <code>timing 100 100</code> while looking at the <b>air</b> view.`,`Optional: <code>calib</code> with the phone 1 m away, then walk to the other side of the room and watch the radar.`],note:`Everything here is what devices broadcast to anyone; the board never connects to them. Still, the list is your neighbours' gadgets: don't post their addresses (they stay out of the committed logs). Phones change their private address about every 15 minutes, so the same phone can show up twice. Distances are guesses from signal strength, good to a factor of two. The ESP32-S3 has Bluetooth LE only: Classic Bluetooth (most audio streaming, older keyboards) is invisible to it.`,preview:{widget:`ble`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`B2`,track:`ble`,title:`Be seen`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`The board shouts its own data`,goal:`In B1 the board listened. Now it becomes a <b>beacon</b>: it packs live values (button presses, the chip's temperature, uptime) into its own 31-byte advertisement, so any phone nearby can read them <i>without connecting</i>. The same budget then carries an <b>iBeacon</b>, an <b>Eddystone-URL</b> or just a name. You choose how often it shouts (the interval) and how loud (TX power), and see what that would cost a coin-cell beacon.`,goalLine:`Make the board broadcast live values that your phone reads without ever connecting.`,goalCheck:[`nRF Connect lists <b>Bench B2</b> and shows <b>Manufacturer data 0xFFFF</b> whose bytes change every second (the uptime) and when you double-click (the presses).`,`The <b>packet</b> view shows 24 of 31 bytes in coloured structures, and the changing bytes flash white.`,`<code>mode ibeacon</code> shows up as an iBeacon on Android (on iPhone apps can't see iBeacons: that's iOS, not you), <code>mode url</code> as an Eddystone URL in nRF Connect.`,`With <code>connectable off</code> and <code>scanresp off</code> the packet view says <b>ADV_NONCONN_IND</b>, nRF Connect no longer offers Connect, and the interval can't go below 100 ms.`,`You can explain from the <b>budget</b> view why beacons advertise every 500-1000 ms and not every 20.`],learn:[`broadcaster role`,`31-byte budget`,`AD structures`,`manufacturer data`,`iBeacon & Eddystone`,`ADV_IND · SCAN_IND · NONCONN`,`interval & advDelay`,`TX power vs battery`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b2 -e ble-lab-b2 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b2 -e ble-lab-b2 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Builds lab B2 and writes it to the board.`,see:`The <b>packet</b> view: <code>bench ADV_IND every 100 ms</code> and two rows of coloured cells.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`uv run pio device monitor`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Opens the serial monitor.`,see:`<code>+ bench ADV_IND every 100 ms, 24+7 B</code> and the command list.`,fix:``},{command:`nRF Connect → Scanner → Bench B2 (tap the row, don't connect)`,where:`phone`,card:`bscan`,does:`The phone is the scanner now (one board can't hear itself). Tapping the row opens the advertisement's details.`,see:`Complete Local Name <code>Bench B2</code>, Manufacturer data <code>0xFFFF 0x01…</code>; the last two bytes (uptime) change every second. Tap RAW to see all 24 bytes.`,fix:`Not listed: pull down to refresh; <code>adv on</code>. On iPhone, Manufacturer data shows as one hex string: the same bytes.`},{command:`(double-click the button) or press`,where:`button`,card:`bscan`,does:`Counts a press into the packet. The board replaces the advertisement data; the controller sends the new bytes from the next event on.`,see:`<code>- presses 1: in the next packet</code>; on the packet view two cells flash white; in nRF Connect byte 4-5 of the manufacturer data becomes <code>01 00</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`bytes`,card:`bnone`,does:`Prints both packets structure by structure.`,see:`<code>[02 01 | 06] Flags</code>, <code>[09 09 | 42 65 6E 63 68 20 42 32] Name</code>, <code>[0A FF | FF FF 01 01 00 2E 10 2C 01] Manufacturer data</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`mode ibeacon`,card:`bscan`,does:`Apple's layout: 4C 00 02 15, a 16-byte UUID, major, minor, the power at 1 m. 30 of 31 bytes: the name moves to the scan response.`,see:`<code>+ ibeacon ADV_IND every 100 ms, 30+10 B</code>. Android nRF Connect: “iBeacon”, UUID BA5E0000-B1E5-…, Major 1, Minor 2. B1 on another board would show the beacon kind in violet.`,fix:`iPhone shows nothing special, or not the beacon: iOS filters iBeacon frames out of every app's scan. Only Core Location (region monitoring) sees them.`},{command:`mode url, then url https://github.com/`,card:`bscan`,does:`Eddystone-URL: service data FEAA with a compressed URL (https:// = 1 byte, .com/ = 1 byte).`,see:`nRF Connect shows <b>Eddystone URL</b> https://github.com/. A URL too long: <code>x the URL doesn't fit…</code>, and the old one stays.`,fix:`Android used to pop these up as notifications (Physical Web); that's gone since 2018. Apps still decode them.`},{command:`connectable off, then scanresp off`,card:`bscan`,does:`First ADV_SCAN_IND (may be scanned, not connected), then ADV_NONCONN_IND: a pure broadcast that answers nobody.`,see:`The packet view's title changes; nRF Connect's Connect button disappears; the scan response row empties.`,fix:`<code>interval</code> raised to 100 ms: the floor for non-connectable adverts (Bluetooth 4.x rule; 5.0 allows 20, beacons stay at 100+).`},{command:`interval 20, interval 1000`,card:`bscan`,does:`How often the board shouts. Click to the <b>air</b> and <b>budget</b> views for each.`,see:`<code>+ … every 1000 ms</code>; nRF Connect's interval column (Android) or the RSSI graph updates slower; the budget's coin-cell life jumps from weeks to years.`,fix:`<code>interval 20</code> refused with connectable off: switch connectable on first.`},{command:`power -12, power 9`,card:`bscan`,does:`TX power in dBm (the shell's built-in command). Lower = shorter range and less current.`,see:`nRF Connect's RSSI drops by about 20 dB at the same distance; the name mode's TX power field changes too.`,fix:``},{command:`adv off (or a long press)`,card:`bscan`,does:`Stops advertising. The board is invisible.`,see:`<code>- advertising off: the board is invisible</code>; nRF Connect greys it out after a few seconds.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`Build an advertisement`,widget:`b2Builder`,intro:`Every field you add costs its data plus 2 bytes (length and type). Put each one in the advertisement, in the scan response (sent only when a scanner asks), or leave it out, and watch the 31-byte budget fill. The board's four modes are presets.`},{title:`When does a scanner hear it?`,widget:`b2Timing`,intro:`The board sends each advertising event on channels 37, 38 and 39 in a row, every interval plus a random 0-10 ms. A phone listens on one channel at a time, for a window out of each scan interval. Slide the numbers and count what gets caught. Switch the random delay off to see why it exists.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`bleScreens`,intro:`Four views. A click goes to the next one; a double-click anywhere counts a press into the packet; a long press switches advertising on or off.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as B1. Flash <code>ble-lab-b2</code> and open the monitor. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> <code>bench ADV_IND every 100 ms, 24+7 B</code>.`,`Install nRF Connect (or LightBlue) on your phone and find <b>Bench B2</b>; tap it open, don't connect. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> Manufacturer data 0xFFFF with bytes that change every second.`,`Double-click the board's button a few times. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> the press counter changes on the phone without any connection.`,`Try <code>mode ibeacon</code> (Android shows it) and <code>mode url</code>. Compare with the builder above: which fields moved to the scan response, and why?`,`<code>connectable off</code>, <code>scanresp off</code>. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> ADV_NONCONN_IND, no Connect button on the phone.`,`Click to <b>air</b> and <b>budget</b>, then try <code>interval 1000</code> and <code>power -12</code>. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> you can say what each costs and what it buys.`],note:`Anyone nearby can read what the board broadcasts: never put anything private in an advertisement. 0xFFFF is the company ID the Bluetooth SIG reserves for tests, fine on a bench, not for a product. iPhone apps can't see iBeacon frames at all (iOS removes them from scans), so test that mode on Android or with B1 on a second board. The budget view is an estimate for a typical coin-cell beacon chip with its stated assumptions: this ESP32-S3 board itself draws about 80 mA with the screen on, far too much for a coin cell. The one radio can't hear itself, so the phone is the scanner in this lab.`,preview:{widget:`ble`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`B3`,track:`ble`,title:`Connect & talk`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Your phone connects, reads and writes`,goal:`Advertising is shouting; a <b>connection</b> is a private conversation. The phone (the <i>central</i>) connects to the board (the <i>peripheral</i>), and from then on they meet on a fixed beat, the connection interval, hopping over 37 data channels. On the board sits a small database, the <b>GATT</b> table: the Bench service with LED, Message, Button, Temperature and Uptime. From nRF Connect or LightBlue you write the LED's colour, put a message on the screen, read the chip's temperature and subscribe to the button.`,goalLine:`Control the board from your phone over Bluetooth, and see every operation arrive on the gatt view.`,goalCheck:[`nRF Connect (or LightBlue) shows <b>Bench B3</b>, connects, and lists the Bench service with five named characteristics plus Device Information and Battery.`,`Writing <code>FF5000</code> to <b>LED</b> turns the on-board LED orange, and the LED row flashes blue on the gatt view.`,`Writing text to <b>Message</b> shows it big on the message view.`,`After you subscribe to <b>Button</b>, every click on the board arrives on the phone as a notification; the Temperature notifies every 2 s.`,`The <b>link</b> view shows the connection interval, MTU and RSSI, and <code>params slow</code> visibly changes the interval.`],learn:[`central & peripheral`,`GATT: services & characteristics`,`UUIDs (16 & 128-bit)`,`read · write · notify`,`CCCD (subscribing)`,`MTU`,`connection interval`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b3 -e ble-lab-b3 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b3 -e ble-lab-b3 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Builds lab B3 and writes it to the board.`,see:`The <b>gatt</b> view with five rows; the radio icon shows rings: advertising.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`uv run pio device monitor`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Opens the serial monitor.`,see:`<code>- advertising as "Bench B3"</code>, then the command list.`,fix:``},{command:`nRF Connect → Scanner → Bench B3 → Connect`,where:`phone`,card:`bscan`,does:`Your phone connects. Install nRF Connect for Mobile (Nordic, free; iPhone and Android) or LightBlue. On iPhone, never connect from Settings → Bluetooth: apps do it.`,see:`Monitor: <code>+ connected: 5A:3C:…</code> and <code>- interval 30.00 ms …</code>, soon <code>- MTU 255: 252 bytes of data per packet</code>. The radio icon turns into two rings, the LED steady blue.`,fix:`Bench B3 not listed: scroll, or filter by name “Bench”. Still nothing: <code>adv on</code>.`},{command:`LED → write → 0xFF5000 (byte array)`,where:`phone`,card:`bconn`,does:`Writes 3 bytes: red FF, green 50, blue 00.`,see:`<code>&lt; write LED FF 50 00</code>; the on-board LED glows orange; the LED row flashes blue and shows the colour swatch.`,fix:`<code>LED wants 3 bytes, got 1</code>: choose Byte Array, not Text or UINT8.`},{command:`Message → write → Text “hello bench”`,where:`phone`,card:`bconn`,does:`Writes UTF-8 text (up to 40 bytes).`,see:`<code>&lt; write Message "hello bench"</code>; click to the <b>message</b> view: big text, “written 3s ago”.`,fix:`Accents show as boxes: the screen font is plain ASCII.`},{command:`Button → subscribe (the ↓↓↓ / bell icon), then click the board's button`,where:`phone`,card:`bconn`,does:`Subscribing writes 01 00 into the Button's CCCD. Each gesture then pushes a 3-byte value: gesture, count (2 bytes).`,see:`<code>&lt; subscribe Button (CCCD = 1)</code>, then per click <code>&gt; notify Button: click #3</code>; the phone shows <code>01-03-00</code>. A bell appears on the Button row.`,fix:`<code>(nobody subscribed)</code> in the monitor: subscribe first.`},{command:`Temperature → read, then subscribe`,where:`phone`,card:`bconn`,does:`Reads 2 bytes (°C × 100, little-endian). The 2904 descriptor tells apps how to show it.`,see:`nRF Connect shows <code>41.42 °C</code> (raw <code>2E-10</code>); subscribed, it updates every 2 s.`,fix:``},{command:`params slow, then params fast`,card:`bconn`,does:`Asks the phone for a 100-200 ms interval (and to let the board skip 4 events), then for 15-30 ms. The phone decides.`,see:`<code>- new params: 112.50 ms, latency 4</code>; the dot on the <b>link</b> view slows down. Notifications now arrive in bursts.`,fix:`<code>params</code> rejected or ignored: iPhones refuse some values; try again after a few seconds.`},{command:`disconnect`,card:`bconn`,does:`Hangs up. The board advertises again by itself.`,see:`<code>! disconnected 0x16: we hung up</code>; the link view keeps the reason.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`From advert to conversation, step by step`,widget:`b3Seq`,intro:`What happens when you tap Connect in nRF Connect, and when you write the LED or the board notifies a click. Each step shows the real ATT bytes: the first byte is the opcode, the next two the handle (the row number in the board's table), the rest the value.`},{title:`The Bench service, live`,widget:`b3Table`,intro:`The board's GATT table as nRF Connect shows it. Press an operation to send it: the packet crosses over, the board's row lights like on the gatt view, and the bytes are decoded underneath.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`bleScreens`,intro:`Four views. A click goes to the next view; every gesture (click, double-click, long press) is also sent to a subscribed phone as a Button notification. A double-click on the gatt and message views sends an extra click.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as B1. Flash <code>ble-lab-b3</code>, open the monitor. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> <code>advertising as "Bench B3"</code>.`,`On your phone, install nRF Connect (or LightBlue), find <b>Bench B3</b> and connect. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> <code>connected</code> in the monitor and the Bench service with LED, Message, Button, Temperature and Uptime on the phone.`,`Write <code>FF5000</code> to LED and a text to Message. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> the LED is orange, the message is on the screen.`,`Subscribe to Button and Temperature, then click the board's button. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> the values arrive on the phone; bells on the gatt view.`,`Click to the <b>link</b> view and try <code>params slow</code> / <code>params fast</code> and <code>phy 2m</code>. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> the interval changes on screen.`,`Type <code>led auto</code> to give the LED back to the radio status, then <code>disconnect</code>.`],note:`Anyone nearby can connect to Bench B3 and change the LED: there is no pairing yet, everything travels in plain text. That's B4. iPhones cache a device's GATT table: if a later lab changes the table and the iPhone shows old rows, turn Bluetooth off and on. The ESP32-S3 has one radio: B3 doesn't use Wi-Fi, so there is no coexistence to worry about.`,preview:{widget:`ble`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`B4`,track:`ble`,title:`Pair & trust`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Make the link private, and remember who's allowed`,goal:`In B3 anyone nearby could connect and read every byte. B4 adds a <b>Vault</b> characteristic that the board only serves over an <b>encrypted, authenticated</b> link. Touch it from the phone and <b>pairing</b> starts: the passkey appears big on the TFT, or both screens show the same six digits and you confirm with the button. Afterwards the board <b>bonds</b>: it keeps the keys in flash, recognises your phone behind its changing private address, and can refuse everyone else.`,goalLine:`Pair your phone with each of the three methods, read the Vault, and lock the board to your phone.`,goalCheck:[`With <code>pair passkey</code>, reading the Vault on the phone brings up a code prompt, the TFT shows six digits, and after typing them the phone reads <b>the bench's secret: 42</b>.`,`The <b>ceremony</b> view fills phase by phase up to <b>bonded</b>, and the header shows a violet lock and an amber key.`,`With <code>pair justworks</code> (after <code>forget all</code> and forgetting the board on the phone) the link is encrypted but the Vault is still refused: you can explain why.`,`With <code>pair compare</code> you accept the matching numbers with a click on the board and a tap on the phone.`,`After <code>allow bonded</code>, your phone still connects, and a second phone (or the same phone after forgetting the board) can't.`],learn:[`pairing vs bonding`,`IO capabilities`,`Just Works · Passkey · Numeric Comparison`,`LE Secure Connections (ECDH)`,`MITM protection`,`LTK & IRK`,`private addresses`,`filter accept list`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b4 -e ble-lab-b4 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b4 -e ble-lab-b4 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Builds lab B4 and writes it to the board.`,see:`The <b>ceremony</b> view: “Waiting for a phone to connect...” and eight grey phases.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`uv run pio device monitor`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Opens the serial monitor.`,see:`<code>- pairing: Passkey · lock off · anyone may connect</code>, then the command list. The method is saved in flash: it survives a reflash of B4.`,fix:``},{command:`nRF Connect → Bench B4 → Connect → Vault → read`,where:`phone`,card:`bconn`,does:`The Vault refuses a plain-text link with “Insufficient Authentication”; the phone reacts by starting pairing.`,see:`The phone asks for a code; the TFT shows <b>PASSKEY ENTRY</b> and six digits; the monitor <code>! passkey 482915: type it on the other device</code>. The ceremony view's phases 1-5 fill, “check” pulses amber.`,fix:`No prompt on iPhone: read the Vault again. On Android the prompt may be a notification.`},{command:`type the six digits on the phone`,where:`phone`,card:`bconn`,does:`Both sides check the passkey bit by bit (20 rounds of confirm and random values), then encrypt and swap keys.`,see:`<code>+ secured: authenticated, bonded, key 16 B</code>, <code>- identity: 4C:…</code>; the phone reads <b>the bench's secret: 42</b>. Lock and key icons in the header; the ceremony track is complete.`,fix:`<code>x … confirm failed: wrong passkey</code>: a typo. Read the Vault again for a new code.`},{command:`disconnect, then connect again and read the Vault`,where:`phone`,card:`bpair`,does:`A bonded phone has the keys: no ceremony, encryption starts straight away.`,see:`The ceremony view says <b>stored keys: bonded before, no ceremony</b> and jumps from connect to encrypted.`,fix:``},{command:`bonds`,card:`bpair`,does:`Lists the bonded devices by identity address (the IRK resolves the phone's private address to it).`,see:`<code>1 bonded device …</code> and <code>1  4C:…  random static</code> (iPhones and most Androids use a random static identity). The <b>bonds</b> view marks it “&lt;- here”.`,fix:``},{command:`forget all, then forget Bench B4 on the phone`,card:`bpair`,does:`Deletes the bonds on the board. The phone still has its half: forget the device in Settings → Bluetooth (iPhone: ⓘ → Forget This Device; Android: the gear → Unpair; macOS: right-click → Forget).`,see:`<code>! forgot every bond</code>, <code>- now forget "Bench B4" on the phone too</code>. If you skip the phone part: <code>x ENC_CHANGE failed 0x206: key missing: one side forgot the bond</code>.`,fix:``},{command:`pair justworks, then read the Vault`,card:`bconn`,does:`The board now says “no input, no output”: nobody can check anything. The link gets encrypted, but not authenticated.`,see:`<code>+ secured: Just Works, bonded</code>, a violet lock, but the Vault read fails again with Insufficient Authentication: the Vault demands MITM protection.`,fix:`That's the lesson: Just Works stops eavesdroppers, not a man in the middle.`},{command:`pair compare (forget first), read the Vault, then click`,where:`button`,card:`bconn`,does:`Both screens show the same six digits. A click on the board says yes, a long press no; the phone has its own button.`,see:`<b>NUMERIC COMPARISON</b> on the TFT, the same number on the phone; after both yes: <code>+ secured: authenticated, bonded</code>.`,fix:`A long press (no) ends with <code>numbers didn't match: said no</code>.`},{command:`allow bonded`,card:`bpair`,does:`Only bonded identities go on the filter accept list; the controller resolves each private address with the stored IRKs and refuses the rest.`,see:`<code>+ only bonded phones may connect (saved)</code>. Your phone connects; another one (or yours after forgetting) gets no connection at all.`,fix:`Locked yourself out: <code>allow anyone</code> over serial.`},{command:`lock on`,card:`bnone`,does:`LED and Message also need an encrypted link. Permissions are part of the GATT table, built once at start-up, so the board saves the setting and restarts.`,see:`<code>lock on saved. … the board restarts now...</code>; afterwards writing the LED from a non-paired phone triggers pairing too.`,fix:`iPhone shows old permissions: turn Bluetooth off and on (it caches the table).`}],panels:[{title:`Which pairing method? It depends on both sides`,widget:`b4Matrix`,intro:`At the start of pairing each side says what it can do: show a number, type one, press yes or no, or nothing at all. The two answers decide the method. Pick the phone's and the board's capabilities (the board's are what <code>pair</code> sets) and see who shows and who types.`},{title:`One pairing, packet by packet`,widget:`b4Seq`,intro:`The Security Manager Protocol (SMP) messages that cross the air when the phone reads the Vault, with their real bytes. Each method differs only in the middle: how a person proves nobody sits in between.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`bleScreens`,intro:`Five views. A click goes to the next one; a double-click runs the action in the footer (secure, bonds, press). While numbers wait for comparison, the button answers instead: click = yes, long press = no.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as B3. Flash <code>ble-lab-b4</code> and open the monitor. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> <code>pairing: Passkey</code> in the start-up lines.`,`Connect from nRF Connect and read the <b>Vault</b>. Type the six digits from the TFT. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> the phone reads the secret; the ceremony view is complete; lock and key in the header.`,`Disconnect and reconnect. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> “stored keys”: no passkey this time.`,`<code>forget all</code> and forget the board on the phone. <code>pair justworks</code>, read the Vault. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> encrypted, yet refused. Why?`,`Forget on both sides again, <code>pair compare</code>, read the Vault and click to confirm. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> authenticated and bonded.`,`<code>allow bonded</code>. <b>Checkpoint 6:</b> your phone connects; after forgetting it on the phone, it can't. Finish with <code>allow anyone</code>.`],note:`Pairing has two halves: whenever you <code>forget</code> on the board, forget the board on the phone too (iPhone: Settings → Bluetooth → ⓘ → Forget This Device; Android: Bluetooth → gear → Unpair; macOS: System Settings → Bluetooth → right-click → Forget), or the next connection fails with “key missing”. If only the phone forgot, NimBLE accepts the new pairing and replaces the old bond by itself (repeat pairing). The method, lock and allow settings are saved in flash (namespace b4) and survive restarts. NimBLE doesn't report the features, method and key-exchange phases one by one: the ceremony view draws them as inferred (a ring) once a later phase proves they happened. The board ships with the secret “the bench's secret: 42”; <code>vault &lt;text&gt;</code> changes it until the next restart.`,preview:{widget:`ble`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`B5`,track:`ble`,title:`Phone app`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Your own app, straight to the board`,goal:`nRF Connect is a screwdriver; now you build the tool. <b>esp-labs/apps/ble-app</b> is a small React app that uses <b>Web Bluetooth</b>: the browser itself becomes the GATT client, so a web page on your Mac or phone talks to the board with no Wi-Fi, no server and no app store. The firmware keeps B3's Bench service and adds four characteristics: a <b>Canvas</b> you draw on from the phone, <b>Sensors</b> that stream five times a second, a <b>Speed</b> test that pushes notifications as fast as the radio allows, and <b>Link</b> controls to change the connection while you watch the speed move.`,goalLine:`Control and draw on the board from a web page, and measure how many bytes per second your link really carries.`,goalCheck:[`The app's Connect tab lists the Bench service with LED … Uptime plus Canvas, Sensors, Speed and Link (ba5e0010-0013), and its byte log shows every operation.`,`A stroke drawn in the Sketch tab appears on the board's <b>sketch</b> view within a fraction of a second.`,`The Live tab charts four lines; touching a jumper wire on GPIO 1 makes the touch line jump.`,`A 50 kB speed run ends with a kB/s figure in the app and the same run as a bar on the board's <b>speed</b> view; after <b>Fast interval</b> or <b>2M PHY</b> the next run is visibly faster (or you can say why not).`],learn:[`Web Bluetooth`,`GATT client in JavaScript`,`write without response`,`binary protocols`,`notification streams`,`throughput = MTU × packets ÷ interval`,`2M PHY & long packets`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b5 -e ble-lab-b5 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b5 -e ble-lab-b5 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Builds lab B5 and writes it to the board.`,see:`The <b>sketch</b> view: “Open the ble-app, connect”. The radio icon shows rings: advertising as <b>Bench B5</b>.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`cd esp-labs/apps/ble-app && pnpm install && pnpm dev`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Installs the app once (React, Vite, TypeScript, exact versions) and serves it at <code>http://localhost:5177</code>. No server code: Vite only hands the page to the browser.`,see:`<code>➜ Local: http://localhost:5177/</code>`,fix:`Port busy: another lab's app runs; stop it or <code>pnpm dev --port 5178</code>.`},{command:`open http://localhost:5177 in Chrome → Connect`,where:`app`,card:`bscan`,does:`The browser's own device chooser opens (a page can never connect by itself). It lists boards with the Bench service, names starting with “Bench”, or BLE-MIDI instruments.`,see:`Pick <b>Bench B5</b>. The Connect tab shows the GATT explorer; the monitor prints <code>+ connected: …</code>. The first time, macOS asks to allow Chrome to use Bluetooth.`,fix:`“Web Bluetooth isn't available here”: use Chrome or Edge, and localhost (not 127.0.0.1 or the Mac's IP).`},{command:`Control tab: drag the colour wheel, send a message`,where:`app`,card:`bconn`,does:`Each move writes 3 bytes to LED (throttled to one write per 80 ms); Send writes up to 40 bytes of UTF-8 to Message.`,see:`The LED follows your finger; <code>&lt; write LED 1F 5C 99</code> lines in the monitor and blue packets crossing the app's air strip.`,fix:`LED stays put after <code>led auto</code>: move the wheel again, the board claims the LED back.`},{command:`Sketch tab: draw`,where:`app`,card:`bconn`,does:`Strokes go to Canvas (ba5e0010) as <b>writes without response</b>: <code>02 cc cc w n</code> + 3 bytes per point, every 50 ms. The board queues them in the NimBLE task and draws them in loop().`,see:`The same lines on the board's <b>sketch</b> view; at the bottom <code>412 cmds 96 writes 9120 B</code>.`,fix:`<code>DROPPED</code> on the board: you drew faster than 16 queued writes; nothing breaks, a few strokes are missing. <code>sketch: a command was cut off</code>: the app fell back to 20-byte writes (MTU 23).`},{command:`Live tab (then touch the wire on GPIO 1)`,where:`app`,card:`bconn`,does:`Subscribes to Sensors: 12 bytes every 200 ms (temperature, touch, free heap, RSSI, a sequence number).`,see:`Four moving lines in the app and the same four sparklines on the board's <b>live</b> view; “notifying the app: 230 sent”. Touching the wire's free end makes the touch value jump by thousands.`,fix:`Touch line flat: GPIO 1 has no wire; any jumper works, the bare metal end is the sensor.`},{command:`Speed tab: 50 kB → Run`,where:`app`,card:`bconn`,does:`Subscribes to Speed and writes <code>01 50 C3 00 00</code>. A small task on the board notifies 50,000 bytes in (MTU − 3)-byte packets until the stack's buffers are full, waits, and continues; then a 15-byte summary.`,see:`Monitor: <code>&lt; write Speed: start 50000 bytes</code> then <code>+ speed: 50000 B in 2310 ms = 21.6 kB/s</code>. The app shows a bar with the same order of magnitude.`,fix:`<code>speed: subscribe to Speed (ba5e0012) first</code>: open the Speed tab once before running from serial.`},{command:`Speed tab: Fast interval, 2M PHY, Long packets → Run again`,where:`app`,card:`bconn`,does:`Writes 1, 3 and 5 to Link: the board asks the central for a 7.5–15 ms interval, the 2M PHY and 251-byte radio packets.`,see:`<code>- new params: 15.00 ms …</code>, <code>- PHY now 2M / 2M</code>; the next run is faster. The board's <b>speed</b> view keeps one bar per run with MTU, interval and PHY.`,fix:`No change: the central decides. macOS often keeps 15 ms and allows 2M; some phones refuse 7.5 ms or 2M. That is the lesson: the peripheral can only ask.`},{command:`pnpm tunnel → open the https address on your phone`,where:`mac`,card:`bscan`,does:`A cloudflared quick tunnel gives the app an <code>https://…trycloudflare.com</code> address, because phones only allow Web Bluetooth on https.`,see:`Android: Chrome opens it and the same chooser appears. iPhone: open it in the free <b>Bluefy</b> browser (Safari has no Web Bluetooth).`,fix:`Bluefy says the page isn't secure: use the https address, not http.`}],panels:[{title:`One colour change, from JavaScript to the LED`,widget:`b5Seq`,intro:`What happens between <code>navigator.bluetooth.requestDevice()</code> in the app and the LED changing colour, and how a notification finds its way back into a chart. Each step shows the real call or the real bytes on air. Pick a flow, then step with ◀ ▶.`},{title:`How fast can it go?`,widget:`b5Throughput`,intro:`Throughput is decided by four numbers: how big each packet may be (MTU), how often the two radios meet (the connection interval), how many packets fit in one meeting, and how fast the bits fly (PHY). Move the sliders: the timeline shows three connection events with every packet drawn to scale, and the result is the same formula the Speed tab measures.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`bleScreens`,intro:`Six views. Click for the next one; double-click on sketch clears it, on speed it starts a 50 kB run, on gatt it sends a Button click. Every gesture also reaches the app as a Button notification, as in B3.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as B1 (screen, button optional). Optional: one jumper wire in GPIO 1, free end loose: your touch sensor.`,`Flash <code>ble-lab-b5</code>. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> the sketch view and the advertising rings.`,`Start the app (<code>pnpm install</code> once, then <code>pnpm dev</code>), open <code>http://localhost:5177</code> in Chrome and Connect to Bench B5. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> the GATT explorer lists nine Bench characteristics.`,`Colour wheel, message, then draw something in Sketch. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> your drawing is on the board.`,`Open Live and touch the wire. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> the touch line jumps in the app and on the board.`,`Speed: run 50 kB, then Fast interval + 2M PHY + Long packets and run again. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> two bars; write down both kB/s figures and the interval each run used.`,`Optional: <code>pnpm tunnel</code> and repeat from your phone (Chrome on Android, Bluefy on iPhone). Compare its speed with the Mac's.`],note:`Still no pairing: anyone near can connect, draw and read the sensors. B4's lock works the same here if you combine them. The app only ever sees boards you pick in the browser's chooser, and the browser hides some standard services from web pages on purpose. iPhones and Macs cache a device's GATT table by its address: if the explorer shows B3's old rows, turn Bluetooth off and on. Throughput depends mostly on the central: the same board is often 2-4× faster with a Mac than with a phone.`,preview:{widget:`ble`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`B6`,track:`ble`,title:`Keyboard & media`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`A Bluetooth keyboard and a remote for your music`,goal:`The board becomes a real Bluetooth keyboard: the same HID service every wireless keyboard has, so phones, Macs and PCs use it with their own driver. Its button turns into a remote: play/pause and skip the music on your phone (which can be playing on your JBL speaker), change the volume, click through slides, or type text you send from the serial monitor. You write the Report Map that tells the computer what our bytes mean, and you see why a keyboard never sends letters, only key numbers.`,goalLine:`Pair the board as a keyboard, control your music with the button, and type text in the right layout.`,goalCheck:[`Your phone or Mac lists <b>Bench B6</b> as a connected keyboard, and the screen's status bar shows the lock (encrypted) and the key (bonded).`,`A click on the media view pauses and resumes your music; a double-click skips the track; touching GPIO 2 turns it up.`,`<code>type yes</code> types “yes”, not “zes”: <code>layout</code> matches the computer's keyboard setting.`,`On the keys view you can read a press as bytes: modifier bits, key number, and the all-zero release after it.`],learn:[`HID over GATT`,`report map`,`key numbers vs characters`,`keyboard layouts`,`consumer control`,`bonding`,`media remote`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b6 -e ble-lab-b6 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b6 -e ble-lab-b6 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Builds lab B6 and flashes it (no new downloads: NimBLE came with B1).`,see:`<code>ble-lab-b6: NimBLE 2.5.1 up</code>, <code>advertising as keyboard "Bench B6"</code>, the command list. The screen opens on the media view: “pair me: Bluetooth settings &gt; Bench B6”.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`Bluetooth settings → Bench B6 → Connect / Pair`,where:`phone`,card:`bpair`,does:`Your phone or Mac connects, reads the Report Map, asks to pair (HID needs encryption), and stores a bond so it reconnects by itself next time.`,see:`<code>+ connected</code>, then <code>+ secured: Just Works, bonded, key 16 B</code>. The status bar shows the linked rings, a violet lock and a key. A Mac may open the Keyboard Setup Assistant: close it, or press the key it asks for and pick ISO (German) or ANSI (US).`,fix:`Listed but won't connect: <code>forget</code> here AND “Forget This Device” on the phone, then pair again. A bond kept on only one side is the usual cause.`},{command:`(media view) click · double-click · touch GPIO 2`,where:`button`,card:`bhost`,does:`Start music on the phone (to its speaker, earbuds or your JBL). Click = play/pause, double-click = next track, hold under 2 s = mute, touch the wire on GPIO 2 / 1 (after <code>touch on</code>) = volume up / down.`,see:`The icon ripples, a dot runs board → phone → speaker, and the music reacts. The monitor prints <code>&gt; media key: play/pause (usage 0CD)</code>.`,fix:`Nothing happens? The app must have audio focus: play something first. On a Mac, Music/Spotify react; a browser tab may not.`},{command:`type hello bench`,card:`bhost`,does:`Open Notes (or any text field) on the paired device, then type this in the monitor. The board queues 2 reports per character (press, release) and sends 6 per frame, 8 ms apart.`,see:`“hello bench” appears on the phone or Mac. The typer view fills its progress bar, then says <code>typed 11 keys</code>.`,fix:`Only the line says <code>not paired yet</code>? Pair first. Letters come out wrong: see <code>layout</code>.`},{command:`layout de (or layout us)`,card:`bhost`,does:`Tells the board which layout the COMPUTER is set to, so it picks the right key for each character. Saved in NVS.`,see:`<code>layout German (QWERTZ) (saved)</code>. Now <code>type yes</code> gives “yes” on a German Mac. Characters the layout needs AltGr/Option for (@ [ ] { } \\ | ~) are skipped with a warning.`,fix:`Still y/z swapped: the computer's layout is the other one. Check the input menu in the Mac's menu bar.`},{command:`key cmd+space`,card:`bhost`,does:`One key or a combo: modifiers (cmd, ctrl, alt, shift) plus a key (enter, tab, esc, arrows, f1-f12, a letter).`,see:`Spotlight opens on the Mac (on an iPhone with a keyboard, the search too). The keys view shows modifier byte <code>08</code> (left GUI) and key <code>2C</code> (space).`,fix:`Windows: <code>key win+r</code> opens Run, <code>key ctrl+alt+delete</code> the security screen.`},{command:`snippet my@mail.example (then double-click on typer)`,card:`bhost`,does:`Saves a text in NVS; a double-click on the typer view (or <code>snippet</code> alone) types it. A tiny macro pad.`,see:`<code>snippet saved</code>, and the text on the typer view.`,fix:``},{command:`(hold the button 2 s) → slides view`,where:`button`,card:`bhost`,does:`Open a presentation (Keynote, PowerPoint, Google Slides in present mode). Click = right arrow (next), double-click = left arrow, hold under 2 s = b (black screen).`,see:`The slide on the screen slides in from the side you moved to; the deck on the computer follows.`,fix:``},{command:`(click to the keys view)`,where:`button`,card:`bhost`,does:`The last reports, byte by byte: the modifier bits, the key numbers, and what each press types with the board's layout.`,see:`<code>down mod 02 key 04 types 'A'</code> then <code>up all zero</code> for a capital A.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`A key press, from button to letter`,widget:`b6Report`,intro:`Type in the box or press a key below and follow it: the board turns the character into a key number with ITS layout setting, sends 8 bytes, and the computer turns the number back into a character with ITS layout. Set the two layouts differently and type <code>yes</code>: that's the bug <code>layout de</code> fixes.`},{title:`Why the board can't play to your speaker`,widget:`b6Sound`,intro:`Your JBL plays music over Classic Bluetooth audio. The ESP32-S3 only has Bluetooth LE, so it can't stream to it, but it can be the remote for the phone that does. Press a media key to follow it, or try the direct path to see where it fails.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`bleScreens`,intro:`Six views. Media and slides are modes: there the button is the remote (click, double-click, hold), and a 2-second hold moves on. On the other views a click moves on as usual.`}],steps:[`Same screen wiring. Optional: two jumper wires in GPIO 1 and GPIO 2 with the other ends loose (touch keys for volume). Flash <code>ble-lab-b6</code>.`,`On your phone or Mac, open Bluetooth settings and pair <b>Bench B6</b>. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> lock and key in the status bar, <code>secured: Just Works, bonded</code> in the monitor.`,`Play music on the phone (to your JBL if you like). On the media view: click, double-click. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> the music pauses, resumes and skips.`,`Type <code>touch on</code> (hands off the wires), then touch the GPIO 2 wire. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> the volume goes up.`,`Open Notes, set <code>layout</code> to match the computer, and <code>type yes, this is the bench</code>. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> exactly that text appears.`,`Hold the button 2 s to reach slides, open a presentation, click through it.`,`Click to the keys view and read one press: modifier bits, key number, then the release.`],note:`While an iPhone or iPad is connected to a hardware keyboard, its on-screen keyboard hides: disconnect the board (or switch Bluetooth off) to get it back. To unpair, forget the board on both sides (<code>forget</code> here, “Forget This Device” there). The ESP32-S3 has Bluetooth LE only: it can't stream audio to a speaker or headphones (that's Classic Bluetooth, A2DP), so the board controls the phone and the phone plays to your JBL. Volume and track keys go to whichever app has audio focus. Hosts disagree on what Option/AltGr characters live where, so the German table only types what it can type the same way on Mac and Windows.`,preview:{widget:`ble`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`B7`,track:`ble`,title:`Music`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`A Bluetooth MIDI instrument`,goal:`The board becomes a MIDI instrument over Bluetooth LE, using the standard BLE-MIDI service that GarageBand, Audio MIDI Setup, Android synth apps and Chrome all understand. MIDI carries notes, not sound: the synth on the other side makes the sound (and can send it to your JBL). Play with the button, five touch wires, serial commands and a 16-step drum machine; notes played on the other side light the board's piano strip and its LED. You see how notes are packed with timestamps, and why the connection interval sets the latency.`,goalLine:`Play notes from the board into a synth, and see the synth's notes on the board.`,goalCheck:[`GarageBand (or the ble-app Music tab) lists <b>Bench B7</b> and makes a sound when you click the button.`,`<code>tune twinkle</code> plays the tune; <code>beat house</code> + <code>seq on</code> plays drums on channel 10.`,`Notes you play on the synth's keyboard light the board's piano strip in blue, and the LED takes each note's colour.`,`The link view shows your connection interval, and you can say how long a note may wait for the radio.`],learn:[`MIDI messages`,`BLE-MIDI packets`,`timestamps`,`running status`,`connection interval = latency`,`capacitive touch`,`General MIDI drums`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b7 -e ble-lab-b7 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b7 -e ble-lab-b7 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Builds lab B7 and flashes it.`,see:`<code>advertising as MIDI instrument "Bench B7"</code>, the command list, the keys view with five keys and a piano strip.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`Mac: Audio MIDI Setup → Window → Show MIDI Studio → Bluetooth icon → Bench B7 → Connect`,where:`phone`,card:`bhost`,does:`macOS connects to the board as a MIDI device (it may ask to pair: accept). Then open GarageBand with a software instrument: it hears every MIDI device.`,see:`<code>+ connected</code>, <code>asked for a 7.5-15 ms interval (low latency)</code>, <code>+ a synth is listening (subscribed)</code>. The keys view says “synth listening”.`,fix:`iPhone/iPad: GarageBand → open a keyboard → settings (gear) → Advanced → Bluetooth MIDI Devices → Bench B7. Android: a BLE-MIDI app (e.g. “MIDI BLE Connect”) plus any synth. Chrome: the ble-app Music tab.`},{command:`(click the button)`,where:`button`,card:`bhost`,does:`Plays the next note of the scale (300 ms). Double-click = a chord. Hold under 2 s = all notes off.`,see:`A sound from GarageBand; the key lights violet on the piano strip; the roll draws a violet bar.`,fix:`No sound but “synth listening”: in GarageBand, pick a software instrument track (not audio) and turn the volume up.`},{command:`tune twinkle (or ode, frere)`,card:`bhost`,does:`Schedules a whole public-domain tune at the current tempo. loop() sends each note when it's due.`,see:`<code>tune twinkle: 14 notes, 8.4 s</code> and the tune from the synth.`,fix:``},{command:`beat house, then seq on`,card:`bhost`,does:`The drum machine: 4 drums x 16 steps on MIDI channel 10, where General MIDI keeps the drums (36 kick, 38 snare, 42 hi-hat, 39 clap).`,see:`The sequencer view's playhead walks the grid; GarageBand plays drums (pick a drum kit track for the real sound).`,fix:`Drums sound like piano notes: the track's instrument isn't a drum kit.`},{command:`touch on`,card:`bhost`,does:`Five jumper wires on GPIO 1, 2, 6, 13, 14 (loose ends) become keys of the pentatonic scale. Hands off while it measures the baseline.`,see:`<code>touch on: 5 keys, pentatonic from C4</code>; touching a wire end lights its key and plays the note, a firmer touch = louder.`,fix:`GPIO 13 and 14 are the RC522's wires from the RFID track: unplug the reader, or use the first three keys.`},{command:`(play the synth's own keyboard)`,where:`app`,card:`bhost`,does:`GarageBand (or the ble-app piano) sends MIDI back to the board as writes.`,see:`Blue keys on the piano strip, blue bars on the roll, the LED in the note's colour, <code>&lt; note E4 vel 96 ch 1</code> lines.`,fix:`On a Mac, GarageBand doesn't send to MIDI devices: use the ble-app piano, or a DAW with MIDI output.`},{command:`(click to the link view)`,where:`button`,card:`bhost`,does:`Explains the latency: notes wait for the next connection event.`,see:`Your interval (7.5-15 ms if the host agreed, often 11.25 or 15 on Apple devices) and a press dropping to the next tick.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`One note, byte by byte`,widget:`b7Packet`,intro:`Play the little keyboard (click, or the keys A to K on your computer). Notes pressed within one connection event share one packet. Watch the header, the timestamps, the status byte and running status, the trick that drops repeated status bytes.`},{title:`Why notes wait: the connection interval`,widget:`b7Latency`,intro:`A connected device may only talk at connection events, one every interval. Pick an interval and play: each note waits for the next event. Then compare a synth that plays notes when they arrive with one that uses the timestamps.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`bleScreens`,intro:`Five views. On keys, sequencer and roll the button is the instrument (click a note, double-click a chord or start/stop the drums, hold for all notes off, hold 2 s to move on).`}],steps:[`Same screen wiring. Optional: five jumper wires in GPIO 1, 2, 6, 13, 14 (unplug the RC522 first). Flash <code>ble-lab-b7</code>.`,`Connect from GarageBand (iPhone/iPad), Audio MIDI Setup + GarageBand (Mac), a BLE-MIDI app (Android) or the ble-app Music tab (Chrome). <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> “synth listening” on the keys view.`,`Click the button a few times, then double-click. <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> notes and a chord from the synth.`,`<code>tune ode</code>, then <code>beat funk</code> and <code>seq on</code>. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> a tune and a drum groove.`,`Play the synth's keyboard. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> blue keys and a coloured LED on the board.`,`Open the link view and read your interval. Try <code>bpm 200</code> with the drums and listen: does it still feel tight?`],note:`MIDI is instructions, not audio: the board never makes a sound itself (it has no speaker). The synth's audio can go anywhere it can play, your JBL included, through the phone or Mac's own Bluetooth. Apple hosts usually grant 11.25-15 ms connection intervals for MIDI, so a note waits up to one interval; the timestamps inside each packet let a good synth keep the rhythm anyway. If the Mac asks to pair, accept: the board allows Just Works bonding. Only public-domain tunes are built in.`,preview:{widget:`ble`}},{kind:`lesson`,id:`B8`,track:`ble`,title:`Host`,status:`built, not flashed`,heading:`Swap roles: your keyboard types onto the board`,goal:`Until now a phone or a laptop was the central and the board answered. Here the board is the central, the way your laptop is. It finds connectable devices, connects, and explores their GATT tables (read-only: only the public basics like name, maker and battery). Then it hosts your own BLE keyboard and mouse: it pairs (the board shows a passkey, you type it on the keyboard), reads the keyboard's own description of its reports (the Report Map), subscribes, and turns the raw bytes into text and a paint cursor on the screen.`,goalLine:`Pair your BLE keyboard with the board, type on its screen, and understand every byte the keyboard sends.`,goalCheck:[`<code>host</code> ends with <code>+ hosting: keyboard media keys</code> (or <code>mouse</code>), after you typed the passkey on the keyboard.`,`What you type appears on the <b>keyboard</b> view; Shift, Ctrl, Alt and Cmd light up while held; <code>layout de</code> swaps Y and Z and shows ä ö ü ß.`,`On the <b>reports</b> view you can name every coloured byte: modifiers (amber), key codes (teal), mouse X (blue), Y (violet), wheel (coral).`,`After a power cycle the board reconnects to the bonded keyboard by itself, no passkey.`],learn:[`central role`,`service discovery`,`HID over GATT (HOGP)`,`Report Map parsing`,`passkey entry as central`,`CCCD subscribe`,`key codes vs layouts`,`bond & reconnect`],pins:[{pin:`3V3`,idx:0,role:`VCC`,side:`L`,kind:`power`},{pin:`7`,role:`BLK`,side:`L`},{pin:`8`,role:`RES`,side:`L`},{pin:`9`,role:`DC`,side:`L`},{pin:`10`,role:`CS`,side:`L`},{pin:`11`,role:`SDA`,side:`L`},{pin:`12`,role:`SCL`,side:`L`},{pin:`GND`,idx:21,role:`GND`,side:`L`,kind:`gnd`},{pin:`5`,role:`BTN`,side:`L`,kind:`btn`},{pin:`0`,role:`BOOT`,side:`R`}],boot:!0,cmd:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b8 -e ble-lab-b8 -t upload`,commands:[{command:`uv run pio run -d labs/ble-lab-b8 -e ble-lab-b8 -t upload`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Builds lab B8 and writes it to the board. NimBLE-Arduino is already downloaded (B1).`,see:`<code>ble-lab-b8: NimBLE 2.5.1 up, address …</code>, the command list, and the <b>devices</b> view filling up.`,fix:`“Failed to connect”: hold BOOT, tap RST, release BOOT, run it again.`},{command:`uv run pio device monitor`,where:`mac`,card:`bnone`,does:`Opens the serial monitor (and restarts the board).`,see:`<code>Bluetooth lab B8 Host (ble-lab-b8)</code> and the commands.`,fix:``},{command:`put the keyboard in pairing mode`,where:`hands`,card:`bperiph`,does:`A keyboard already paired to your Mac only talks to the Mac. Pairing mode makes it advertise to anyone: usually hold the Bluetooth or channel key 3 s until its light blinks fast. Multi-channel keyboards: pick a channel your Mac isn't using.`,see:`The <b>devices</b> view: your keyboard near the top with a keyboard icon and <b>HID</b>.`,fix:`Not there: it is still connected to the Mac (switch channel, or remove it in the Mac's Bluetooth settings). Apple Magic Keyboards use Classic Bluetooth and never show up here.`},{command:`list`,card:`bperiph`,does:`Prints the connectable devices, strongest first. Beacons are left out: they can't be connected to.`,see:`<code>  1   -48 dBm  keyboard/mouse  C4:7D:2A:91:0E:33  Bench Keys  [HID]</code>`,fix:``},{command:`connect 1`,card:`bperiph`,does:`Sends CONNECT_IND to device 1 (now the board chooses the connection timing), then explores: discovers every service, characteristic and descriptor and reads the public basics.`,see:`<code>&gt; CONNECT_IND to Bench Keys</code>, <code>+ connected: we are the central</code>, <code>+ 5 services, 19 characteristics in 820 ms</code>, <code>- it is a keyboard/mouse (1812): type host</code>. The <b>explore</b> view shows the tree; HID values say “locked until paired”.`,fix:`<code>no answer from …</code>: it stopped advertising (pairing mode times out after ~60 s): start it again.`},{command:`host (or a long press)`,card:`bperiph`,does:`Pairs (bond + passkey + Secure Connections), reads the Report Map, finds each input report through its 2908 descriptor and subscribes (writes 01 00 into its CCCD).`,see:`Six big digits on the screen and <code>! type 482915 on the keyboard, then Enter</code>. Type them on the keyboard, press Enter: <code>+ secured: passkey, bonded, key 16 B</code>, <code>&lt; Report Map: 72 bytes, 11 input fields</code>, <code>&gt; CCCD 01 00: report 1 (keyboard)</code>, <code>+ hosting: keyboard media keys</code>. The view jumps to <b>keyboard</b>.`,fix:`<code>pairing failed</code>: the keyboard left pairing mode, or it still has an old bond with the board: <code>forget</code>, remove it on the keyboard side (pairing mode again), <code>connect</code>, <code>host</code>.`},{command:`type on your keyboard`,where:`hands`,card:`bperiph`,does:`Each key press is a notification: the board compares it with the previous one and adds the newly pressed keys. Backspace deletes, Enter starts a new line, Esc clears.`,see:`Your text on the <b>keyboard</b> view with a blinking cursor; the modifier caps light amber while you hold Shift, Ctrl, Alt or Cmd; the media keys show a violet toast.`,fix:`Wrong letters (y and z swapped)? See the next command.`},{command:`layout de`,card:`bhost`,does:`Key codes are positions, not letters. The board turns them into characters with the layout you choose: US or German.`,see:`<code>- layout German (QWERTZ): the same keys, other letters</code>. Now the key printed Z gives z, and ä ö ü ß appear.`,fix:``},{command:`(click to the reports view)`,where:`button`,card:`bhost`,does:`The raw input reports, newest at the bottom, each byte coloured by the Report Map field it belongs to.`,see:`<code>#1 kbd 02 00 0B 00 00 00 00 00</code> with 02 amber (Left Shift) and 0B teal (the H key), and the decoded line under it.`,fix:``},{command:`boot on, then host`,card:`bhost`,does:`Boot protocol: the fixed 8-byte format every BIOS understands, without reading the Report Map. Compare with report protocol.`,see:`<code>&gt; CCCD 01 00: report 0 (boot keyboard)</code> and <code>bootK</code> rows on the reports view. <code>boot off</code> switches back.`,fix:`<code>no Protocol Mode: this device has no boot mode</code>: some keyboards don't offer it.`},{command:`disconnect, then the mouse: pairing mode, connect <n>, long press`,where:`hands`,card:`bperiph`,does:`A mouse has no keys and no screen, so pairing falls back to Just Works (no digits). Its Report Map describes buttons, X, Y and the wheel with whatever bit sizes it likes.`,see:`<code>+ secured: Just Works, bonded</code>, <code>+ hosting: mouse</code>, and the <b>mouse</b> view: left button draws, right changes colour, middle clears, the wheel sets the brush.`,fix:`The cursor races across: that mouse reports in large steps; the board already halves them.`},{command:`unplug and replug the board`,where:`hands`,card:`bperiph`,does:`The bond (keys) is saved in NVS. When the keyboard advertises again (press a key to wake it), the board recognises its address and reconnects.`,see:`<code>+ bonded Bench Keys is back: reconnecting</code>, then <code>+ secured: …</code> with no passkey, and <code>+ hosting</code>.`,fix:`Keyboards that hide behind a changing private address (RPA) may not be recognised: <code>connect</code> + <code>host</code> by hand then.`},{command:`connect <n> on your JBL speaker`,card:`bperiph`,does:`Explore a device that isn't a keyboard. Only the public basics are read, nothing is written.`,see:`A few services, mostly one long vendor UUID, and <code>- JBL app's own service only: audio uses Classic BT</code>. The audio itself never goes over BLE on this speaker, and this chip has no Classic Bluetooth.`,fix:`Not in the list: it only advertises BLE while the JBL app is looking, or right after power-on.`},{command:`forget`,card:`bnone`,does:`Deletes every bond on the board.`,see:`<code>- every bond deleted: pair again to use a keyboard</code>. Remove the board on the keyboard side too (pairing mode), or it will refuse the next pairing.`,fix:``}],panels:[{title:`From scan to keystroke, step by step`,widget:`b8Flow`,intro:`Everything that happens between your keyboard advertising and an <b>h</b> appearing on the board, packet by packet. Pick a story: the first pairing, coming back after a power cycle, or exploring your JBL speaker. Use ◀ ▶ or let it play; the box below each step shows the real bytes.`},{title:`Decode a report yourself`,widget:`b8Decoder`,intro:`A keyboard never sends letters, only key positions and bits. Pick a Report Map, then press keys on the little keyboard or drag on the mouse pad: the report flies to the board and every field lights up in turn, with the Report Map item that defined it. Switch US/DE: the same key code becomes another letter.`},{title:`What the screen shows`,widget:`bleScreens`,intro:`Seven views. A click goes to the next one, a double-click runs the action at the bottom left, a long press takes the next step: connect, then host, then disconnect.`}],steps:[`Same wiring as every BLE lab (the screen; the push button is optional). Flash <code>ble-lab-b8</code> and open the monitor.`,`Put your BLE keyboard in pairing mode on a channel the Mac isn't using. <b>Checkpoint 1:</b> it shows up on the <b>devices</b> view with <b>HID</b>.`,`<code>connect 1</code> (or double-click to pick it, then a long press). <b>Checkpoint 2:</b> the <b>explore</b> view lists 1800, 180A, 180F and 1812 Human Interface Device with its Report characteristics.`,`<code>host</code> (or a long press). Type the six digits on the keyboard and press Enter. <b>Checkpoint 3:</b> <code>+ hosting: keyboard</code> and the <b>keyboard</b> view.`,`Type a sentence, hold Shift, try Backspace and Enter. If you have a German keyboard: <code>layout de</code>. <b>Checkpoint 4:</b> your text on the TFT, umlauts included.`,`Click to <b>reports</b> and name each coloured byte; compare with “Decode a report yourself” above. Then <code>boot on</code>, <code>host</code>: 8 fixed bytes.`,`Optional: <code>disconnect</code>, pair your mouse the same way and paint on the <b>mouse</b> view.`,`Unplug and replug the board, then press a key on the keyboard. <b>Checkpoint 5:</b> it reconnects and hosts without a passkey.`,`Explore your JBL speaker: <code>connect &lt;n&gt;</code>. See why it shows nothing about audio.`],note:`Explore only connects to devices you pick and only reads the standard public values (name, appearance, maker, model, firmware, battery, PnP ID); it never writes to a device, except the two standard HID settings on a keyboard or mouse you host (the CCCD to subscribe, Protocol Mode for boot). Only connect to your own devices. One link at a time: disconnect before connecting to the next device. A keyboard bonded to the board may stop working with the Mac on that channel until you pair it there again. The keyboard view uses the screen's built-in font, which has ä ö ü Ä Ö Ü ß but no €. BLE keeps the report ID out of the notification: it lives in the Report Reference descriptor (2908), which is why the bytes on the reports view start with the data.`,preview:{widget:`ble`}}];export{e as bleLessons};
